    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's helpful. one more thing—when we're talking about these \"complexity and management\" issues, specifically with data consistency and session persistence, are there any common \"unseen\" or unexpected errors that usually pop up once you actually start scaling out? like small things that can wreck the system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, some common unseen issues with scaling out include:\n\n* \"Dependent sessions\" where changes made on one server affect another server's session state, even if the user navigated from one to another.\n* \"Cache inconsistencies\" resulting from differing values between servers, leading to stale data or incorrect data being displayed.\n* \"Insertion point\" issues where adding a new server can disrupt the existing flow of data, introducing new bottlenecks.\n\nThese kinds of issues often require additional monitoring, logging, and testing to identify, and may require changes to the system's architecture or data models to resolve.",
      "source": "target",
